    "title": "LEKTION 29: BEEN ODER GONE",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Zwei Partizipien von go, zwei Bedeutungen:",
          "",
          "BEEN = war dort und ist zurück.",
          "  I have been to the bank. (wieder da)",
          "GONE = ging dort hin und ist noch weg.",
          "  He has gone to the bank. (noch weg)",
          "",
          "\"Have you ever been to...?\" — so fragt",
          "Englisch nach einer Lebenserfahrung.",
          "",
          "In schneller Rede verkürzt sich der Helfer:",
          "  I haven't been.                    Drücke 1",
          "  You've never been?                 Drücke 2",
          "  He's gone out.                     Drücke 3",
          "  Haven't you gone yet?              Drücke 4"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I haven't been.",
          "2": "You've never been?",
          "3": "He's gone out.",
          "4": "Haven't you gone yet?"
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Die kleinen Wörter des Perfect, je ein Job:",
          "",
          "ever    — je, irgendwann? (in Fragen)",
          "never   — nie",
          "already — früher als erwartet (schon)",
          "yet     — bis jetzt (Frage/Verneinung)",
          "just    — gerade eben",
          "",
          "Die meisten stehen in der Mitte, vor dem",
          "Partizip: \"I have never seen it.\"",
          "Aber yet steht am Ende:",
          "\"Have you finished yet?\"",
          "",
          "Deutsch setzt schon, nie, gerade recht",
          "frei — Englisch strenger: Mitte, yet ans Ende."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "030",
    "trigger_word": "test",
    "trigger_card_number": 676,
    "title": "LEKTION 30: PERFECT ODER PAST",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Das Zeitwort wählt die Zeitform.",
          "",
          "ABGESCHLOSSENE ZEIT (vorbei) → past simple:",
          "yesterday, last week, ago, in 2019",
          "",
          "  I saw the film yesterday.          Drücke 1",
          "  She called twice last week.        Drücke 2",
          "  We spoke two days ago.             Drücke 3",
          "",
          "OFFENE ZEIT (läuft noch) → present perfect:",
          "today, this week, ever, so far",
          "",
          "  I have seen the film this week.    Drücke 4",
          "  She has called twice today.        Drücke 5",
          "  So far we have spoken twice.       Drücke 6"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "I saw the film yesterday.",
          "2": "She called twice last week.",
          "3": "We spoke two days ago.",
          "4": "I have seen the film this week.",
          "5": "She has called twice today.",
          "6": "So far we have spoken twice."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "FOR und SINCE messen die offene Zeit:",
          "for + Dauer:  \"for ten years\"",
          "since + Start: \"since Monday\"",
          "",
          "Achtung: Deutsch sagt für beides seit —",
          "Englisch trennt: for (Dauer), since (ab).",
          "",
          "Gleiche Handlung, zwei Zeiten:",
          "\"I lived here for years.\" (vorbei)",
          "\"I have lived here for years.\" (noch)",
          "",
          "Dein Perfekt ist die normale",
          "Vergangenheit. Im Englischen NICHT: das",
          "Zeitwort entscheidet — lies es zuerst."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
